course: dictionary
lesson: 4005
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which one you need. The word gar can be a fish, a sea fish in the UK and Australia, or a short piece of a longer word. Read the context to decide.
explain: «Anglers in the USA catch alligator gar, which can be huge.» Anglers are people who fish, and alligator gar is a big fish, so here gar means a fish. Check by swapping in your meaning: a fish fits the sentence, but a piece of a word would not.
explain: Gar is also a chunk inside longer words. In «Without its cédille, garçon would be read "gar-KON"», the letters gar start the word garçon, and in «GAR-den, gardening» they start the word garden. Seeing the chunk helps you read the word, but it does not mean a fish there.
explain: Gars are old kinds of fish with hard, shiny scales. People call them «living fossils» because their kind has changed very little for a very long time.
fact: A gar is a long, thin fish with a narrow snout full of sharp teeth.
fact: In the UK and Australia, «garfish» is a long, thin sea fish with a beak-like jaw.
fact: «Gar» is also a short piece of longer words, like «garden» or «cigar».
fact: Gars have hard, shiny scales, and are called «living fossils».
fact: Alligator gar is a huge fish that anglers catch in the USA.
exercise: Anglers in the USA catch «alligator gar, which can be huge». What is a gar here? | A small piece of a word | A bird of the sea | A type of tree | *A kind of fish
exercise: «Without its cédille, garçon would be read "gar-KON".» In this sentence, what is gar? | A kind of fish with sharp teeth | A sea fish with a beak-like jaw | *A short chunk at the start of a longer word | A spotted cat
exercise: What does «garfish» mean in the UK and Australia? | A large pond bird | A short piece of a word like garden | A hard, shiny scale | *A long, thin sea fish with a beak-like jaw
test: Why are gars called «living fossils»? | They can fly over the water | They are made of stone | *Their kind is very old and has changed little | They only live in the sea
test: In «GAR-den, gardening», what is gar? | A long, thin fish | *A short chunk of letters at the start of the word | A sharp tooth | A kind of tree
exam: «The gar hid in the river, showing its sharp teeth.» What does gar mean here? | A type of cigar | A garden hose | A short piece of a word | *A long, thin fish with a narrow snout
